2026-06-27 19:05:42,211 [INFO] ("Based on the definition of 'Religious Level' as the hierarchical complexity within religious organizations, identify the range of religious hierarchy levels present in the polity <polity> during the period from <time-start> to <time-end>. Your answer should reflect the number of distinct hierarchical levels within the most complex religious organization(s) present, and be provided in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>].", None, [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You are are history professor writing challenging questions for PhD students.\n\n    Your task is to create a challenging yet well-defined historical exam question based on a variable definition from a historical dataset.\n    Follow the rules below, and return ONLY the final question template, based on the examples provided. \n\n    1. Begin the question with a concise explanation of the variable's definition, strictly adhering to the provided description, if no description is provided, use the variable name as the basis for the question.\n    2. Ensure all of the following template markers are used:\n        - '<polity>' for polity names (e.g. 'The Papal States')\n        - '<time-start>' and '<time-end>' for temporal scope\n    3. The question should make abundantly clear what the format of the desired response is and that only that response should be given.\n    4. Specify the answer unit if applicable, but require that the final answer does not contain it.\n    5. The answer format should be requested as: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>], just like a json array\n\n    Provided variables, and description will always relate to a numeric value, so ensure that questions refer to identifying a range.\n    Structure your response with a clear question template, and template markers.\n    "}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "\n                        Variable name: administrative level\n                        Variable unit: not applicable\n                        Variable description: Talking about Hierarchical Complexity, Administrative levels records the administrative levels of a polity. An example of hierarchy for a state society could be (1) the overall ruler, (2) provincial/regional governors, (3) district heads, (4) town mayors, (5) village heads. Note that unlike in settlement hierarchy, here you code people hierarchy. Do not simply copy settlement hierarchy data here. For archaeological polities, you will usually code as 'unknown', unless experts identified ranks of chiefs or officials independently of the settlement hierarchy. Note: Often there are more than one concurrent administrative hierarchy. In the example above the hierarchy refers to the territorial government. In addition, the ruler may have a hierarchically organized central bureaucracy located in the capital. For example, (4)the overall ruler, (3) chiefs of various ministries, (2) midlevel bureaucrats, (1) scribes and clerks. In the narrative paragraph detail what is known about both hierarchies. The machine-readable code should reflect the largest number (the longer chain of command).\n                    "}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Given the concept of Hierarchical Complexity in terms of Administrative Levels, identify the maximum number of administrative levels for the polity <polity> during the period from <time-start> to <time-end>. An example of hierarchy for a state society could be (1) the overall ruler, (2) provincial/regional governors, (3) district heads, (4) town mayors, (5) village heads. Note that unlike in settlement hierarchy, here you code people hierarchy. Provide your answer in the format [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>].'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: military level\n                        Variable unit: not applicable\n                        Variable description: Talking about Hierarchical Complexity, Military levels records the Military levels of a polity. Same principle as with Administrative levels. Start with the commander-in-chief coded as: level 1, and work down to the private. Even in primitive societies such as simple chiefdoms it is often possible to distinguish at least two levels – a commander and soldiers. A complex chiefdom would be coded three levels. The presence of warrior burials might be the basis for inferring the existence of a military organization. (The lowest military level is always the individual soldier).\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Using the definition of 'Military Level' as a measure of hierarchical complexity within a military structure, identify the range of military levels present in the polity <polity> between <time-start> and <time-end>. Your response should reflect the levels of hierarchy from the commander-in-chief to the individual soldier and should be expressed as a JSON array in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>]."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: polity territory\n                        Variable unit: square kilometres\n                        Variable description: Talking about Social Scale, Polity territory is coded in squared kilometers.\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Based on the concept of 'Polity Territory' as defined in terms of the area a polity controlled in squared kilometers, provide the range of the area <polity> governed from <time-start> to <time-end>. Please provide your answer in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>], without the unit."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: religious level\n                        Variable unit: Not provided\n                        Variable description: _Zoroastrianism_ "or more exactly Mazdaism"<br>Third-century CE Zoroastrian two priests were highly influential in the development of Zoroastrianism as the Sasanid state religion (three if we include Pabag, the founder of the Sasanian dynasty, who was a priest).<br>Kerdir "may be considered the father of the Zoroastrian church in this period, as he was the one who attempted to make Zoroastrianism into a uniform body, with a unified doctrine, attached to the state."<br>"The Sasanian sources state that Tosar was responsible for the codification of the Avesta ... Kerdir brought about the organization of the church and a religious hierarchy."<br>1. King of Kings (until Shapur II)<br>2. Ohrmaz mowbed (chief priest) mowbedan mowbedmowbedan mowbed was the "head of the religious order"<br>Kerdir was one powerful Zoroastrian priest, caretaker of the Anahid fire temple at time of Wahram II (274-293 CE).<br>"When Sassanid kings were raised to the throne they received the insignia of royal authority from the chief Mobedh who held the highest religious office."<br>3. mowbed (district level) (head priest)"important functions and carried out legal as well as religious and administrative duties.<br>4. mow/mogThe magus (mow/mog) had a higher status and later was also involved in economic and legal matters. Above him was the chief magus (mowbed), who held an important position and was probably the main religious authority throughout the empire."<br>4-5. herbeds (teacher priests)"instructed the people in daily ritual, prayer, and tradition and tended the fire."<br>"Three major fire-temples were established for the three classes ... Smaller fire-temples existed in the villages and towns, attended by a teacher-priest (herbed)."<br>"Magians had a hierarchy parallel to that of the state, a hierarchical judicial administration specifically for Zoroastrians, a cult, scriptures, religious laws, and distinctive customs. It was the religion of the elite and rulers."<br>Eight different priests required for some Zoroastrian rituals e.g. vispered ritual and the videvad sade purification ritual "who took up specific positions in the ritual area, also described in the Nirangestan."<br>haoma-pressing priest (hawanan)<br>fire-lighting priest (atr-wakhsh)<br>presenting priest (frabertar)<br>tending priest, who brings water (abert or danu-uzwaza, which refers to the river Danu)<br>washing priest (asnatar)<br>mingling priest (raethwish-kar)<br>auditing priest (sraoshawarz)<br>one who brings sacrificial animal (pasu-wazah)<br>Comprehensive source on Zoroastrian religion: Moazami (2016) "Zoroastrianism: Religious texts, theology, history and culture."<br>_Nestorian Christianity_<br>"first synod of the Nestorian Church was convened in 410" during reign of Yazdgerd I (399-420 CE). "Persian Christianity became officially recognized and the Nestorian Patriach resided at the royal city of Ctesiphon; he and the Jewish exilarch became responsible for their coreligionists." "The Sasanian state used the churches as intermediaries to regulate and tax the population."<br>1. Patriach<br>"Persian Christianity became officially recognized and the Nestorian Patriach resided at the royal city of Ctesiphon".<br>the Sasanid king "organized a Christian Persian church that grew in number, and many in the royal family and the nobility, especially the women, gravitated toward this religion."<br>2. Catholicos in province"The Christian community was headed by the Catholicos"<br>"The Catholicos in each province oversaw the Christian congregation and provided money and guidance for the community."<br>3. Metropolitan"The Sasanians appointed a catholicos or patriarch and a metropolitan to preside over the bishops in parallel with the Sasanian administrative hierarchy." <br>4. Bishops of Bishoprics"According to al-Biruni, Christianity had reached Merv within 200 years of the birth of Christ and the first reference to a Merv bishopric dates to the year 334."<br>5. Heads of Churches"by the end of the Sasanian period there were churches and bishoprics established throughout the empire, and many from the royal family also converted to Christianity."<br>"Royal permission was required for the election of the heads of churches, for construction of buildings, for burials, and even for the issue of monastic rules."<br>6.<br>_Judaism_<br>1. Exilarch (Resh Galut)<br>2. Rabbis3.<br>_Buddhism_<br>"The Buddhas of Bamiyan and a number of Iranian texts in the Sogdian and Khotanese languages are testaments to the importance of Buddhism in eastern Iran."<br>_Manicheanism_<br>"Manicheans moved east and westward, through some still remained in Iran, to write down their tradition and spread it among all people."<br>Manichaean community in Merv mid-3rd CE.\n                    '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 2231, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 2146})
